
Here’s a concise reverse-engineering profile of **youtube.com** based on publicly visible surface data and official documentation: YouTube is Google’s video platform, its public mission is “to give everyone a voice and show them the world,” and the main public site exposes standard corporate, legal, creator, advertiser, and developer entry points in its footer navigation. [youtube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AO0pwo7UzfY)

## What is directly visible

The homepage footer exposes these top-level public areas: About, Press, Copyright, Contact us, Creators, Advertise, Developers, Terms, Privacy, Policy & Safety, How YouTube works, Test new features, and NFL Sunday Ticket.  The footer also shows YouTube is operated under Google LLC and the fetched page carried the notice “© 2025 Google LLC.” [youtube](https://www.youtube.com/robots.txt)

## Public identity

YouTube’s official “About” page states its mission is “to give everyone a voice and show them the world,” and says it is built around listening, sharing, and community through stories.  That gives you the high-level product positioning: user-generated and publisher video distribution, creator/community focus, and global media reach. [youtube](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AO0pwo7UzfY)

## Crawlable structure

YouTube publishes a `robots.txt` file that disallows crawlers from several application-style paths such as `/api/`, `/comment`, `/get_video`, `/get_video_info`, `/live_chat`, `/login`, `/results`, `/signup`, `/youtubei/`, and multiple AJAX endpoints.  The same file advertises sitemap locations at `https://www.youtube.com/sitemaps/sitemap.xml` and `https://www.youtube.com/product/sitemap.xml`, which signals a very large site with separated discovery/indexing layers. [youtube](https://www.youtube.com/robots.txt)

## Developer-facing capabilities

Google’s developer documentation says the YouTube Data API supports uploading videos, managing playlists and subscriptions, updating channel settings, and searching videos, playlists, and channels.  Google also documents an IFrame Player API for embedding the YouTube player and controlling playback with JavaScript, which confirms a formal external integration surface beyond the main website UI. [developers.google](https://developers.google.com/youtube/v3)

## Practical reverse-engineering notes

From a recon perspective, the publicly confirmed surface suggests several major product domains: consumer watch/browse UI, creator/channel management, policy/legal flows, advertising flows, developer APIs, and partner/sports product areas.  The blocked paths in `robots.txt` also hint at internal or semi-internal app boundaries such as video retrieval, comments, live chat, login/signup, and `/youtubei/`, but `robots.txt` alone does not prove implementation details or security posture. [developers.google](https://developers.google.com/youtube/v3)

